{"timestamp_utc": "2026-08-13T11:10:07.568507+00:00", "trace_schema_version": 3, "run_id": "smoke_v21_full", "arm": "A_full", "case_id": "EKR_GEN_018", "case_type": "ekr_general_question_bank", "source": "qa-ekr.csv", "expected_route": "generic", "expected_agent": "GenericAgent", "expected_building_id": null, "expected_fields": null, "final_route_taken": "generic", "final_agent_invoked": "GenericAgent", "final_building_id_resolved": null, "final_sql_fields_used": [], "final_sql_fields_used_by_agent": {}, "final_vector_chunks_retrieved": [], "final_answer": "Calculating the total power demand in your home involves two main components:  \n 1) Estimating your electrical (appliance and lighting) load  \n 2) Estimating your space-heating (and, if relevant, cooling and hot-water) load  \n\nBelow is a step-by-step method you can follow. You can refine the calculation further if you have data from an Energy Performance Certificate (EPC), for example the heated floor area (\u201cepc_egenatemp\u201d), building type (\u201cepc_egenbyggnadstyp\u201d) or the number of floors (\u201cepc_egenantalplan\u201d).  \n\n1. Inventory your electrical loads  \n\u2022 List every electrical device and fixture in the home (lights, outlets, appliances, electronics).  \n\u2022 Note the rated power (in watts) for each device (usually on the nameplate).  \n\u2022 Estimate a \u201cusage factor\u201d or duty cycle for each device (e.g. 0.2 for a fridge that runs 20% of the time).  \n\u2022 Compute each device\u2019s average power draw:  \n\u2003Device power draw (W) = Rated power (W) \u00d7 Usage factor  \n\u2022 Sum all devices to get your total average electr", "answer_quality_verdict": {"verdict": "pass", "axes": {"faithfulness": null, "answer_relevance": 9, "question_coverage": 10, "calibration": 10}, "composite": 9.666666666666666, "evidence_present": false}, "clarification_fired": false, "request_address_fired": false, "forced_route_applied": false, "evidence_present": false, "total_attempts"