{"timestamp_utc": "2026-08-13T11:10:41.437148+00:00", "trace_schema_version": 3, "run_id": "smoke_v21_full", "arm": "A_full", "case_id": "EKR_GEN_019", "case_type": "ekr_general_question_bank", "source": "qa-ekr.csv", "expected_route": "generic", "expected_agent": "GenericAgent", "expected_building_id": null, "expected_fields": null, "final_route_taken": "generic", "final_agent_invoked": "GenericAgent", "final_building_id_resolved": null, "final_sql_fields_used": [], "final_sql_fields_used_by_agent": {}, "final_vector_chunks_retrieved": [], "final_answer": "Your total electricity cost is not just a simple \u201ckilowatt-hour price\u201d times \u201cused kilowatt-hours.\u201d Instead, your consumption affects several components of what you ultimately pay. Below is a breakdown of the main price elements in a typical Swedish household contract and how your usage level influences each one:\n\n1. Wholesale (spot) price  \n   \u2022 This is the hourly Nord Pool spot price, which you pay roughly one-to-one if you have a spot-based contract.  \n   \u2022 The more you consume during hours when the spot price is high (often cold, wind-still winter mornings or early evenings), the higher your average spot-price cost.  \n   \u2022 Conversely, shifting consumption to low-price hours (e.g. windy, mild nights) lowers your average spot-price component.\n\n2. Retail supplier margin  \n   \u2022 Many electricity retailers add a small fixed \u201cmark-up\u201d per kWh or a fixed monthly fee.  \n   \u2022 Your total mark-up grows proportionally with your consumption (for per-kWh mark-ups) or stays constant (for flat mont", "answer_quality_verdict": {"verdict": "pass", "axes": {"faithfulness": null, "answer_relevance": 9, "question_coverage": 10, "calibration": 9}, "composite": 9.333333333333334, "evidence_present": false}, "clarification_fired": false, "request_address_fired": false, "forced_route_applied": false, "evidence_present": false, "total_attempts": 2, "early_block_exhausted":